# Radio button

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Use radio buttons when exactly one answer can be chosen from a short list. A new unit choice, a display mode and a small set of exclusive options all fit.

Use [Checkbox](checkboxes.html) for several answers and [Dropdown menu and overflow menu](select.html) for a long list. Do not use radios for an immediate on or off control. A switch and a question have different expectations.

This page covers:

- Radio button
  - `<input type="radio">`
  - `ha-radio-group`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A group of radios has a question, which is the legend, and two or more choices. Each choice is a small circle and a label. Exactly one choice is selected at a time, and a person cannot clear it, only change it.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-input-type-radio"><code>&lt;input type="radio"&gt;</code></h3>

The native radio. Radios that share a `name` form a group, and an unnamed radio is a group of one. `value` is the answer sent, `checked` selects it, `disabled` switches it off and `required` means the group must have one choice selected. Its implicit role is `radio`.

Put the group in one `fieldset` with a `legend` that asks the question. Give every radio a `label`, wrapped or linked with `for`. The label is the accessible name and also makes the click target larger.

```html
<fieldset>
  <legend>Which temperature unit should be used?</legend>
  <input id="unit-c" name="unit" type="radio" value="c">
  <label for="unit-c">Degrees Celsius</label>
  <input id="unit-f" name="unit" type="radio" value="f">
  <label for="unit-f">Degrees Fahrenheit</label>
</fieldset>
```

In a native group, Tab moves onto the selected radio, or the first one if none is selected. The arrow keys move to the next or previous radio and select it, and Space selects the focused one. The browser draws the circle, and `accent-color` colours it.

You cannot write a radio in a [Markdown card](markdown-card.html). The card removes the `type` attribute from `input` unless it is a checkbox. In a form or on a dashboard, use the `select` selector instead: `mode: list` with one choice draws a group of radios, and `mode: box` draws a grid of large options that work the same way.

```yaml
select:
  options:
    - Degrees Celsius
    - Degrees Fahrenheit
  mode: list
```

<h3 id="child-ha-radio-group"><code>ha-radio-group</code></h3>

Home Assistant draws a group with `ha-radio-group` and one `ha-radio-option` for each choice. A few older dialogs still use a Material radio list item.

`ha-radio-group` takes `label` and `hint`, which are the question and its hint, plus `name`, `orientation` (`horizontal` or `vertical`), `disabled` and `required`. Home Assistant asks for the label, because the group needs a name. `ha-radio-option` takes `appearance` (`default` or `button`), `size`, `checked` and `disabled`. The `button` appearance gives a larger target, 40 CSS pixels high by default.

You cannot set these in YAML. The `select` selector chooses them for you.

**Theme colours.** The Civica theme colours a radio through its form-control keys. In light and dark mode:

| Part | Theme key | Light | Dark |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Ring, unselected | `ha-color-border-neutral-normal` | #6F777B | #9BA4AA |
| Ring on hover | `ha-color-border-neutral-loud` | #0B0C0C | #FFFFFF |
| Ring and dot, selected | `ha-color-fill-primary-loud-resting` | The theme’s primary colour | The theme’s primary colour |
| Fill, selected | `ha-color-fill-primary-normal-resting` | #E8F1F8 | #12324D |
| Fill on hover | `ha-color-fill-neutral-quiet-hover` | #EDEDED | #222323 |

The ring is 2 pixels wide (`ha-border-width-md`). The unselected ring against the card is 4.56:1 in light mode and 5.86:1 in dark mode, calculated from theme colours. The radio border is the neutral grey, not the near-black of the text fields. The theme also sets older Material radio keys, which affect only the older list item. It does not set the `--ha-radio-option-` names, so size, ring width and the checked-dot scale stay Home Assistant’s defaults.

The selected state is a dot as well as a colour. A disabled radio follows the theme’s disabled colours.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A native group gives you the keyboard behaviour and the role without script.
- Every choice is visible, so people can compare.
- A native radio cannot be written in a Markdown card.
- A dashboard author cannot set the size, orientation or button appearance of Home Assistant’s radios.
- A long list becomes tiring: use a dropdown instead.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

- Use one fieldset and legend, one shared name, distinct values and a label for each radio. See [Fieldset and legend](fieldset.html).
- Keep the list short and the choices visible when comparing them helps.
- Show the saved choice when editing an existing setting.
- For a new, unanswered question, do not select an answer for the person.
- Include a genuine “Use device setting” or “Not sure” choice when it is valid, rather than forcing a false either-or.
- Keep the order the same everywhere, and avoid “Other” without a text field.
- Put follow-up questions after the choice, and do not hide essential explanation behind an option. Put a hint under the legend: see [Help / hint text](hint-text.html).
- On a small screen, stack the choices rather than squeezing several into a row.
- If a choice has an immediate effect, use a switch or a [Button group / segmented control](button-group-segmented-control.html) instead.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the legend and the label tie each choice to its question.
- 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions and 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: the question and each choice say what they are.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value and 2.5.3 Label in Name: the label is the name.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard: a native group works from the keyboard. Test Home Assistant’s group with the keyboard on your own dashboard.
- 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: the unselected ring is above 3:1 in both modes, calculated from theme colours.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the selected radio shows a dot as well as colour.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry and 3.3.1 Error Identification: keep the chosen answer after an error, and tie the error message to the group.

Test with no initial answer, a changed selection, the answer after an error, the arrow keys and long labels. Compare light and dark modes, at least two theme colours and 320 CSS pixels. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

